I was working at a nonprofit called  generation when I met Jasmine jasmine  was only twenty four years old but she  had already been through a lot and life  kept throwing her curveball after  curveball she had held a few jobs off  and on but most were part-time and it  was hard to manage her work schedule  with caring for her one-year-old  daughter she was living with her  daughter's father for a while so that  provided some stability but  unfortunately he developed an alcohol  abuse problem and she began to fear for  her own safety and her daughter's Jazmin  found a community in Atlanta that  provides a safe haven for women who have  escaped abusive situations with the  solid footing of Housing and Safety she  began to look for full-time work  determined to create a better world for  her daughter one that hold the promise  of opportunity that she never had  Jasmine's determination was admirable  she applied to multiple jobs every day  but received interviews from very few  employers the interview she had they  were going so well either  she knew someone had to be hiring but  despite her best efforts no one wanted  to hire Jasmine this story is not  uncommon what I found from my work at  Generation is that while the details may  change similar circumstances are  experienced by many unemployed young  people  now when I say unemployed young people I  mean those aged 18 to 29 who are not in  school and are actively searching for  work the International Labor  Organization estimates that around the  world 75 million young people are  unemployed three times as many are  underemployed this represents not just a  source of individual despair but also a  source of social unrest  as well as a gigantic pool of untapped  talent paradoxically this is a two-sided  problem according to a global report by  McKinsey &amp; Company 40% of employers say  that a skill shortage is driving  entry-level vacancies meaning employers  cannot find the skills that they need in  the labor pool this problem has  persisted for years but it is only now  beginning to be addressed the solution I  propose modeled on this issue is to  create partnerships between the  nonprofit and for-profit worlds that  from the outset align outcomes and  enable stronger collaboration  I believe this proposition is valuable  in many sectors but for the purpose of  illustration let's consider this issue  of unemployment among young people there  are three critical aspects of the  education to employment journey first  enrolment are enough young people being  trained for the job market to building  skills is training giving young people  the right skills and 3 finding a job can  young job seekers find open positions so  why does it not work for nonprofits to  solve this issue alone a nonprofit that  focuses on skills training has many  priorities with limited time and  resources attending to this entire  education to employment journey is  nearly impossible and there are  outstanding questions on all three of  these aspects first enrollment the  nonprofit sector is highly fragmented  and with a donation-based business model  nonprofits cannot operate at this scale  needed to serve all of the young people  who would benefit from their services to  building skills without employer  involvement there is no mechanism to  ensure that the curriculum and delivery  is done in a way that prepares young  people to be successful on the job not  just in the classroom and third finding  a job without commitments from or at  least connections  with employers nonprofits may not be  helping young people with the critical  piece of actually landing a job so now  let's shift gears and consider the other  side of the coin the for-profit world in  this case that means employers why do  employers not solve this issue alone why  don't they just hire young people and  then train them the problem is it's  expensive the employer would have to  invest in developing the right type of  skills training and then when they hire  employees these employees spend a long  time being trained and during that time  they're not producing output for the  company some large employers do have the  capacity to develop training programs  but the reality is most unemployed young  people aren't going to be working for  these resource-rich large companies  additionally many unemployed young  people come from difficult life  situations similar to Jasmine they need  social support structures in place to  help them succeed in their training and  in their transition to the workplace and  this is why nonprofits are also needed  as part of the equation to address the  social issues of unemployment not just  the talent development piece and which  employers are invested in 2015  generation where I worked was launched  to bring together the nonprofit and  for-profit worlds to solve this issue of  unemployment among young people when  Jasmine went through the generation  program she benefitted tremendously from  this model as a non profit generation  was able to provide social support  services such as a mentor and a stipend  to help her cover her transportation and  food cost the program's emphasis on  employer involvement was also key to  Jasmine's success for instance  Generation had worked directly with  employers from the beginning to identify  the key skills and mindsets they needed  and this was the foundation for  Jasmine's training generation has  focused on proving the positive return  on investment of the program as this  return on investment is proven  generations funding sources have become  diverse and include government's  foundations and employers themselves all  stakeholders benefit from this  collaboration governments reduce their  unemployment rates foundations provide  economic mobility for young people and  employers have a pipeline of talent with  reduced turnover and higher productivity  this collaboration is essential to reach  the scale needed to truly move the  needle on unemployment among young  people when Jasmine graduated from the  generation program she received the job  of her dreams she called me and said  something to the effect of I feel like  my life has changed already I want my  daughter when she's grown to look back  and be proud of the life we made I think  that's possible now the program worked  for Jasmine the program has worked for  over 25,000 young people in ninety nine  cities across nine countries and it's  expanding rapidly I had the opportunity  to help launch the generation Hong Kong  program in spring 2018 right before  coming to Stanford here's a photo of  their first graduation ceremony the  program is impactful and scalable and  this would not be possible without the  collaboration among sectors in each  generation location today  I've told you a story about unemployment  among young adults because it's an issue  that matters deeply to me but I'm also  excited to be sharing my story with you  because I want to extend an invitation I  invite you to think about an issue that  matters deeply to you it could be  malnutrition financial insecurity access  to health care or any other issue that  moves you what would be possible if  nonprofit and for-profit organizations  worked hand in hand every  step of the way like they've done for  jasmine and like they've done for  thousands of other unemployed young  people around the world I challenge you  to extend this model to the issue that  matters to you because there are so many  more people in our communities and  around the world that we can help thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

